    <h1>How Roman Numerals Work</h1>
    <p class="lead">Roman numerals use seven letters to write numbers. They still appear on clocks, book chapters, film dates and monuments, so it helps to know how to read them.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>The seven letters</h2>
<p>I is 1, V is 5, X is 10, L is 50, C is 100, D is 500 and M is 1000. Larger numbers are built by adding letters together, so VI is 6 and XV is 15.</p>
<h2>The subtraction rule</h2>
<p>When a smaller letter comes before a larger one, it is subtracted. IV is 4, IX is 9, XL is 40, XC is 90, CD is 400 and CM is 900. Only these six pairs use subtraction.</p>
<h2>Reading 1994</h2>
<p>Split the number into thousands, hundreds, tens and units: 1000 is M, 900 is CM, 90 is XC and 4 is IV. Together they make MCMXCIV. Read it left to right, adding each part.</p>
<h2>Limits</h2>
<p>Traditional Roman numerals have no zero and no symbol for numbers above 3999 in standard form. Repeating a letter is limited to three times, so 4 is IV rather than IIII. Our converter follows these standard forms.</p>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/roman-numeral-converter.html">Roman Numeral Converter</a> converts in both directions and rejects forms that break the rules.</p>
